# secure-local.tflw — testFlow M128a (PLAN_M128_PENTEST_TIER1.md, D293): the executable proof that
# `env secureLocal` is a real target and not just a config block nobody points at.
#
# Why it exists at all, given M128a ships no tflw changes. The pentest arc's rule pack (M128b/M128c)
# is largely a set of claims about a *TLS* response, so `env secureLocal` is the ground every one of
# those claims will stand on. Adding the env and leaving it unexercised until M128c would mean a
# whole milestone in which the target is asserted to work rather than shown to — the exact shape
# this suite keeps finding on the wrong end of (see PROGRESS.md's "two green checks that weren't").
# Two assertions now cost nothing and fail loudly if the sidecar, the cert regeneration or the
# 8443 listener ever stops working.
#
# `expect header "server" matches "nginx"` is only true *through* the sidecar: under the default
# `env local` this file would hit the api directly, get no `Server` header at all, and fail. It
# lived in `tests/.env-specific/` for that reason alone until tflw `M247` (`D1353`) gave the
# language a way to say so — each test is `skip "…" on env local`, so bare discovery reports it
# skipped with the reason, and `secure-local-check` runs it where it means something.
#
#   npx tflw run --env secureLocal tests/api/identity/secure-local.tflw tests/api/identity/ciphers.tflw
#   (or: npm run test:secure-local)

@auth @tls @identityOps @api
test "the 8443 sidecar serves the same api over TLS" skip "only true through the TLS sidecar, which env secureLocal addresses" on env local
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  # Proves the request actually went through nginx rather than resolving to the plaintext backend:
  # Express sends no `Server` header, nginx always does. It is also the response
  # `sec/server-version-disclosure` will fire on in M128b — recorded in VULNS.md as a prediction
  # about the untouched app, and this is where that prediction becomes a running assertion.
  expect header "server" matches "nginx"

@auth @tls @sessions @identityOps @api
test "the session cookie carries Secure over TLS" skip "only true through the TLS sidecar, which env secureLocal addresses" on env local
  # The dogfood-side regression guard for M128a's first real fix. Before it, `res.cookie()` in
  # apiV2/src/auth/auth.service.ts set HttpOnly/SameSite/Max-Age/Path and no `Secure`, so the
  # session cookie came back unprotected over exactly this listener. The flag is conditional on the
  # request scheme (X-Forwarded-Proto, set by nginx/nginx.conf), which is why the plaintext half of
  # this pair lives in scripts/verify-security-target.mjs instead — one `--env` per run, and the
  # negative it asserts is "Secure is ABSENT over http".
  api POST /auth/session-login body { email: env(USER_A_EMAIL), password: env(USER_A_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  expect header "set-cookie" matches "Secure"
  expect header "set-cookie" matches "HttpOnly"

# `S-3d` infrastructure (decision 18): the sidecar offers HTTP/2 by ALPN since `S-3d`. tflw's API
# client negotiates HTTP/1.1 against it, and must get the same answers it got before h2 was on —
# text outside ASCII included, since a framing change is where an encoding goes wrong.
@tls @edge @api
test "an HTTP/2-capable TLS front still answers tflw's client, which speaks HTTP/1.1" skip "only true through the TLS sidecar, which env secureLocal addresses" on env local
  api GET /edge/unicode
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.city equals "Łódź"
  api GET /edge/chunked
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.chunks has count 3

# `T-1e` (the whole-product review's D5, *cookie attributes across hosts*): tflw's jar files a cookie
# under the origin that set it — `scheme://host:port` (SPEC §3.3) — and honours `Domain=` by host.
# The sidecar and the plain listener are two origins of one host and one app, so this is both rules
# on the real stack, and the first run is what said so: `/edge/cookies` sets three host-only cookies
# and one with `Domain=localhost`. The host-only three stay with the TLS origin; the domain cookie
# reaches the same host on another port and scheme, as its `Domain` says. A browser would send all
# four (RFC 6265 ignores ports) — the narrowing is tflw's, deliberate, and now pinned.
@tls @sessions @identityOps @api
test "host-only cookies stay with the origin that set them, and a Domain cookie crosses to the same host on another port" skip "only true through the TLS sidecar, which env secureLocal addresses" on env local
  api GET /edge/cookies
  expect status equals 200
  api GET /edge/cookies/echo
  expect body.cookie contains "edge_secure=3"
  expect body.cookie contains "edge_domain=4"
  api plain GET /edge/cookies/echo
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.cookie equals "edge_domain=4"
